  "verbalization": "Cumingianoside E is implicated in the pathway leading to HPAC through multiple distinct mechanisms. The first pathway links Cumingianoside E to SNU-387, which is a target of Neridienone A, and ultimately to HPAC. The second and third pathways both show that Cumingianoside E targets Phosphatidylserine synthase 1, which is a target of two different compounds, (-) Carveol and [(1R,2R,5S,8R,9R,10S,11R,12S)-9,10-diacetyloxy-5,11-dihydroxy-4-(hydroxymethyl)-8,12,15,15-tetramethyl-13-oxo-2-tricyclo[9.3.1.03,8]pentadec-3-enyl] acetate, which also targets HPAC. These findings suggest that Cumingianoside E may act as a common modulator of Phosphatidylserine synthase 1, which in turn influences HPAC. The evidence supports a potential role for Cumingianoside E in modulating pathways that ultimately affect HPAC, though the exact mechanism of action remains to be fully elucidated. The integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of the relationship between Cumingianoside E and HPAC, emphasizing the need for further experimental validation to confirm the biological relevance of these interactions.",
